@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Operators at a chemical plant see the new process safety programme as extra paperwork. What would best build a shared sense of purpose?
A) Connect the programme to real incidents and to everyone going home safely
B) Explain that the programme is mandatory under corporate policy and will be audited every quarter
C) Offer a bonus to the operators who complete the most safety forms each month
D) Remove some of the programme's checks so that the operators see less paperwork in their daily work
= A
? People commit to a goal when they understand why it matters to them. Linking the programme to real consequences builds purpose far better than rules or rewards.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Developers see security as the security team's job rather than their own. What would best build shared ownership?
A) Make security part of the team's goals and definition of done, with experts in planning
B) Ask the security team to review every story at the end of each sprint and report any problems
C) Send developers a monthly report showing the number of security defects found in their code
D) Keep security separate, since developers should focus on delivering new features to customers
= A
? Shared ownership grows when security is part of the team's own goals and standards, and when experts collaborate with the team from the start.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
Which one-page tool helps a diverse group agree on a project's purpose, scope, stakeholders and main risks at the start?
A) A project canvas
B) A Gantt chart
C) A control chart
D) A burndown chart
= A
? A project canvas summarises the essential elements of a project on one page and is created collaboratively, which builds shared understanding.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Leaders of an airline programme want a dedicated room where goals, progress and problems are visible to everyone involved. What is this practice called?
A) An obeya (big room)
B) A sandbox environment
C) A data warehouse
D) A war chest fund
= A
? An obeya is a lean visual management practice: a room where objectives, progress and problems are displayed so that leaders and teams share the same picture.

@@ PE1 | Agile
A newly formed team keeps arguing about priorities. The coach suggests a short team mission statement. How should it be created?
A) By the team together, linked to the product vision
B) By the product owner alone, before the next sprint
C) By the sponsor, who knows the organisation's strategy
D) By copying the mission statement of another team
= A
? A mission statement that the team creates together, connected to the product vision, builds ownership and helps the team make consistent decisions.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
Team members ask how their project contributes to the organisation's strategy. What should the project manager do?
A) Show how the strategy links to the project's objectives and to their own work
B) Tell them that strategy is a matter for senior management, not for the team
C) Send them a copy of the organisation's annual report to read in their own time
D) Ask the sponsor to answer their questions at the end of the project
= A
? Showing the "line of sight" from strategy to objectives to individual work gives people purpose and helps them prioritise.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid | multi
Which two practices help a distributed security team keep a common vision? (Choose two.)
A) Revisiting the goals regularly in joint planning sessions
B) Making goals and progress visible to every location
C) Letting each location set goals of its own
D) Sharing the vision only with the team leads
E) Presenting the vision once, at the kickoff
= A, B
? A common vision is maintained through regular joint reflection and shared visibility, especially when teams work in different places.

@@ PE2 | Agile
The security team often blocks releases with findings at the last minute, and the developers are angry. What should the project manager do?
A) Bring both teams together to move security checks earlier and agree shared release criteria
B) Ask the security team to stop blocking releases, since delivery dates must always come first
C) Ask the developers to accept the findings, since the security team has the final authority
D) Escalate the conflict to the chief information officer and ask for a decision on who is right
= A
? Collaborating on the root cause, which is late feedback, resolves the conflict. Moving checks earlier and agreeing shared criteria serves both teams' interests.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
A member of the evaluation panel discloses that her spouse works for one of the bidders. What should the project manager do?
A) Remove her from the evaluation and record the disclosure
B) Keep her on the panel, since she has been honest about it
C) Ask her to give that bidder an average score to stay neutral
D) Disqualify the bidder, since a conflict of interest exists
= A
? A disclosed conflict of interest is managed by removing the person from the decision and documenting it. The bidder is not penalised for the panel member's relationship.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Researchers object to new security controls that block software they use in their experiments. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion to find solutions meeting both needs, such as approved exceptions
B) Enforce the controls without exceptions, since security policies apply equally to all university staff
C) Remove the controls for the research departments, since academic freedom is more important than security
D) Ask the researchers to use their own personal laptops for the experiments that need the blocked software
= A
? Collaborative problem solving looks for options that satisfy both interests, such as controlled exceptions with compensating safeguards.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two departments with equal power must quickly reach a temporary agreement on sharing a laboratory. Full collaboration is not possible at the moment. Which approach fits best?
A) Compromising
B) Forcing
C) Smoothing
D) Withdrawing
= A
? Compromising, in which each side gives up something, is appropriate when parties have equal power and a quick, temporary solution is needed.

@@ PE2 | Agile
A conflict between two team members continues despite the scrum master's facilitation, and it is now affecting the whole team. What should the scrum master do?
A) Involve a neutral mediator, such as an experienced coach or HR
B) Move one of the two team members to another team without discussion
C) Ask the rest of the team to vote on who is right in the conflict
D) Ignore the conflict, since the team should resolve it on its own
= A
? When facilitation fails, a neutral third party can help the people involved resolve the conflict before it does further harm.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
In a requirements workshop for a crew scheduling system, union representatives and airline managers argue about how certain roster rules should be interpreted. What should the project manager do?
A) Record both views, refer the dispute to the right forum and keep the rules configurable
B) Choose management's interpretation, since management is paying for the new crew scheduling system
C) Choose the union's interpretation, since the union's members will use the system every day
D) Cancel the workshop until the union and management have resolved all of their disagreements
= A
? The project should not settle labour disputes itself. It documents both views, escalates to the proper forum and keeps the design flexible.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
In a negotiation, the vendor opens with a very high price to influence the final outcome. What is this tactic called?
A) Anchoring
B) Smoothing
C) Fait accompli
D) Good guy, bad guy
= A
? Anchoring uses a first offer to set a reference point. Knowing the market and one's own BATNA helps a negotiator resist it.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Team members disagree about whether cameras must be switched on in video meetings. What should the scrum master do?
A) Help the team discuss its needs and agree a working agreement
B) Require cameras to be on in every meeting from now on
C) Let each person do whatever they prefer, without any discussion in the team
D) Ask the functional managers to decide the rule for the team
= A
? Working agreements created by the team resolve recurring disagreements about how the team works, and people commit to rules they help create.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
During a ransomware incident, many people give conflicting instructions to the response team. What should the leader do first?
A) Establish clear command: one incident lead, defined roles and a regular update rhythm
B) Let everyone continue to act on their own judgement, since speed matters most in an incident
C) Ask the most senior executive present to take over all technical decisions personally
D) Wait for the external forensic consultants to arrive before giving any instructions
= A
? In a crisis, a directive style with clear command, roles and communication rhythm reduces confusion and speeds up the response.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Operators at the plant rarely report near misses because they fear being blamed. What should the project manager do?
A) Promote a just culture: thank reporters, focus on systems and act on reports
B) Make near-miss reporting compulsory and discipline operators who fail to report them
C) Offer a cash reward for every near miss that an operator reports to management
D) Stop collecting near-miss reports, since operators do not want to report them
= A
? A just culture separates honest mistakes from reckless behaviour, makes reporting safe and shows that reports lead to improvements.

@@ PE3 | Predictive | match
Match each motivation theory with its key idea.
1) Maslow
2) Herzberg
3) McClelland
4) Vroom
A) A hierarchy of needs, from basic needs to self-actualisation
B) Hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction, while motivators drive satisfaction
C) Needs for achievement, affiliation and power
D) Motivation depends on expectancy, instrumentality and valence
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Understanding motivation theories helps leaders choose suitable ways to engage different team members.

@@ PE3 | Agile
Engagement is low after a long period of maintenance work. Which activity could renew the team's energy and creativity?
A) A short, timeboxed hackathon on ideas chosen by the team
B) A longer sprint, so that the maintenance work is finished sooner
C) A performance review for each member of the team
D) A rule that maintenance work must be done in the evenings
= A
? Giving the team autonomy to explore its own ideas in a short, focused event can restore motivation and generate useful innovations.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
Key engineers in a three-year programme are being approached by other companies. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss their career goals and offer development opportunities and recognition
B) Ask human resources to add penalty clauses to the engineers' contracts
C) Assign them less interesting work so that they are less visible to recruiters
D) Do nothing, since engineers always change jobs every few years anyway
= A
? Retention depends on growth, recognition and meaningful work. Understanding people's career goals lets the leader address what matters to them.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A developer wants to become a scrum master. How should the current scrum master support her?
A) Agree a development plan, such as facilitating some events with coaching and feedback
B) Tell her to wait until a scrum master position becomes available in another team
C) Suggest that she reads the Scrum Guide on her own and takes a certification exam
D) Discourage her, since the team needs her skills as a developer more than as a scrum master
= A
? Servant leaders support people's growth with practical opportunities, coaching and feedback.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
The project manager realises that she tends to dominate meetings when she is under stress. Which component of emotional intelligence is she demonstrating?
A) Self-awareness
B) Social skills
C) Empathy
D) Motivation
= A
? Self-awareness is recognising one's own emotions and their effect on behaviour. It is the foundation for self-regulation.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid | multi
Which two actions help a leader guide a team through a long period of uncertainty? (Choose two.)
A) Sharing what is known and unknown, honestly and often
B) Focusing the team on what it can control
C) Waiting until everything is certain before communicating
D) Promising outcomes that cannot be guaranteed
E) Avoiding discussion of the team's concerns
= A, B
? Honest, frequent communication and a focus on what the team can influence maintain trust and productivity when much is uncertain.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A remote team member has become quiet and disengaged in recent sprints. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Have a private one-to-one conversation to understand what is going on
B) Mention the problem in the next retrospective in front of the whole team
C) Report the team member's lack of engagement to the functional manager
D) Wait to see whether the team member's engagement improves on its own
= A
? A private, caring conversation is the best first step to understand the reasons for a change in behaviour.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A chemical plant upgrade could change the emergency planning arrangements for the surrounding area. Which stakeholders must be engaged?
A) Local emergency services and the authorities responsible for emergency planning
