@@ PE5 | Hybrid
The board expects the new security programme to make the company completely immune to cyberattacks. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that risk can be reduced but not eliminated, and agree measurable targets
B) Confirm the board's expectation, since the programme has a large budget and expert staff
C) Avoid the subject, since the board members are not experts in cybersecurity
D) Promise immunity for the first year only, while the programme is still running
= A
? Expectations must be realistic. Agreeing measurable targets for risk reduction gives the board a meaningful way to judge success.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
Users expect all 20 years of historical records to be migrated to the new system, but the plan migrates five years and archives the rest. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the approach and how archived data can be accessed, and agree it with the owner
B) Migrate all 20 years of data to avoid disappointing the users, whatever it costs the project
C) Say nothing until go-live, since most users will not need the older records anyway
D) Delete the older records, since they will no longer be needed in the new system
= A
? Expectation gaps are closed by explaining the approach and its reasons, showing how needs will still be met and getting the owner's agreement.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Stakeholders are upset that items labelled "should have" were not delivered in the release. What should the product owner do in future?
A) Clarify what each MoSCoW category means when priorities are set
B) Label every item as "must have" to avoid disappointment
C) Stop using MoSCoW and prioritise items without telling anyone
D) Deliver all "should have" items, whatever the date impact
= A
? MoSCoW only aligns expectations if everyone understands that "should have" items are important but not essential for the release.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
After acceptance, the customer expects new features to be built free of charge under the warranty. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that the warranty covers defects; new features are changes
B) Build the features for free to keep the customer satisfied with the project
C) Refuse to discuss the features until the warranty period has ended
D) Ask the team to build the features in their spare time without charging
= A
? Expectations are aligned against the contract: warranties cover defects, while new functionality is new scope.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Students expect new features during the exam period, but the team has planned a change freeze then. What should the product owner do?
